# Known False Positives for Pre-Push Scan
#
# Each entry describes a pattern the agent should recognize as a false
# positive when reviewing pre-push-scan.sh findings. The agent uses
# semantic matching — descriptions should explain the *reason* a finding
# is benign, not provide a grep pattern.
#
# Format: One description per bullet. Blank lines and comment lines
# (starting with #) are ignored.

- Username or SSH host alias matching against skill or knowledge
  description keywords in index files (e.g., model names like "granite"
  in skills/index.md coincidentally matching an SSH host alias)

- Planted credential fixtures inside
  `skills/agentfs-setup/scripts/test-pre-push-scan.sh`. The test suite
  must contain credential-shaped strings in order to prove the scanner
  detects them — a scanner test with no test vectors proves nothing.
  All values are vendor-published documentation examples or obvious
  fakes (`AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE` is AWS's own docs key;
  `ghp_abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789` is sequential alphabet;
  the PEM entry is a bare `-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----` header
  with no key body). None authenticate against anything.

- Fictional phone numbers inside Category 6's own test vectors in
  `skills/agentfs-setup/scripts/test-pre-push-scan.sh` and the regex
  examples in the Category 6 comment block of `pre-push-scan.sh`.
  All use the 555 exchange reserved for fiction (555-123-4567,
  555-867-5309, (212) 555-0199, +14155552671, 415.555.2671) and
  reach nobody. The scanner must contain phone-shaped strings to
  prove it still catches real ones after the 2026-11-16 regex
  tightening.

- The literal placeholder `sk-litellm-local-no-auth` appearing in
  dsh-* and litellm-* skill files (SKILL.md, setup.sh,
  install-desktop.sh). The local LiteLLM proxy runs without a
  master_key and authenticates nothing; DSH only requires its
  apiKeyEnv variable to be non-empty. Not a credential. Lines
  matching the secrets pattern via `API_KEY=` are false positives.

- The SSH host aliases `rhel-ai` and `rhtevan-work` in hosted-model-ctl,
  skupper-model-provider, goose-skupper-provider and related skill or
  knowledge files (profile HOST fields, DEFAULT_PROFILES keys, CHANGELOG
  and log.md entries, deployment/fitting docs). They are ~/.ssh/config
  aliases required by the profile format, not resolvable hostnames, IPs,
  or usernames. Accepted by the user 2026-11-16.
- The bare SSH host alias `work` (same host as `rhtevan-work`) matching
  the ordinary English word "work" in prose of any skill, knowledge, or
  log file. The scanner greps each alias as a whole word, so plain-text
  uses such as "after the work" trip it. Only the English word is
  allowed — a line that names the host as a connection target (ssh,
  scp, HOST field) is still a finding. Accepted by the user 2026-11-19.
- The SSH host alias `crc-vm` in crc-install (SKILL.md "CRC VM Access (SSH)"
  section, CHANGELOG/log entries). It is a suggested ~/.ssh/config alias for
  the local CRC VM via the user-networking loopback forward (HostName
  127.0.0.1, Port 2222, User core) — not a real host, IP, or username.
  Accepted by the user 2026-11-23.
- The shell variable names `$SECRET` / `$SECRET_EXISTS` (and `$CKEY`)
  in crc-ols scripts (add-provider.sh, install.sh, rotate-key.sh,
  remove-provider.sh) and their echo/log lines. They hold the *name*
  of a Kubernetes secret and its data key, never a credential value.
  The only literal is the placeholder `no-key-required` used by
  --dummy-key for unauthenticated self-hosted/Skupper endpoints.
  Accepted by the user 2026-11-23.
- The `SECRET = re.compile(...)` detector pattern in
  `skills/agentfs-eval/scripts/principles/risk_scan.py` (and similar
  credential-word regexes in the principles lab). It lists words such as
  api_key, secret, password, token, id_rsa so that the risk scan can flag
  scenarios that touch credentials; it contains no credential value.
  Accepted by the user 2026-11-25.
- The test input `git@github.com:x/y` in
  `plugins/plab-jail/tests/test-jail.sh`. It proves the jail blocks
  `git remote add` inside a sandbox; `x/y` is a placeholder, not an
  account or repository. Accepted by the user 2026-11-25.
